namespace DGtal {
//----------------------------------------
/*!
@page modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators Estimators based on maximal segments
@writers Tristan Roussillon

[TOC]

Part of the \ref packageGeometry.

This part of the manual describes how to estimate local geometric
quantities (tangent or normal vectors, curvature...) along a digital curve
from the maximal segments of a given primitive (digital straight segments,
digital circular arcs...).

\section modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators-Overview Overview

The estimation relies on three kinds of objects:

- a segment computer (see \ref moduleGridCurveAnalysis), which recognizes
  the segments of a given primitive along a range of elements (points or
  steps of a digital curve), e.g. ArithmeticalDSSComputer (digital
  straight segments on points), StabbingLineComputer (straight segments
  on steps, i.e. pairs of inner and outer points), StabbingCircleComputer
  (digital circular arcs, DCAs, on steps);

- a segment computer estimator (model of CSegmentComputerEstimator), which
  estimates a quantity at an element from one segment, e.g.
  TangentFromDSSEstimator, TangentAngleFromDSSEstimator,
  CurvatureFromDSSEstimator, CurvatureFromDSSLengthEstimator,
  NormalFromDCAEstimator, TangentFromDCAEstimator, CurvatureFromDCAEstimator
  (see SegmentComputerEstimators.h);

- an estimator (model of CCurveLocalGeometricEstimator), which combines
  the maximal segments passing through each element of the curve, computed
  by a saturated segmentation (SaturatedSegmentation):
  MostCenteredMaximalSegmentEstimator or LambdaMaximalSegmentEstimator.

Both estimators are used in the same way: they are constructed from a
segment computer and a segment computer estimator, initialized with the
whole range of elements (iterators for an open curve, circulators for a
closed curve), and then evaluated either at one element or on a subrange:

@code
typedef ArithmeticalDSSComputer<ConstIterator, int, 4> SegmentComputer;
typedef TangentFromDSSEstimator<SegmentComputer> SCEstimator;
typedef LambdaMaximalSegmentEstimator<SegmentComputer, SCEstimator> Estimator;

Estimator estimator( SegmentComputer(), SCEstimator() );
estimator.init( itb, ite );
// estimation at one element
auto tangent = estimator.eval( it, h );
// estimation on a (sub)range
std::vector<Estimator::Quantity> tangents;
estimator.eval( itb, ite, std::back_inserter( tangents ), h );
@endcode

The evaluation on a (sub)range is faster than the evaluation at each
element, since the maximal segments are computed only once.

\section modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators-MostCentered Most centered maximal segment estimator

MostCenteredMaximalSegmentEstimator assigns to each element the quantity
estimated from one maximal segment passing through it, which is called
the most centered maximal segment (see mostCenteredMaximalSegment).
It is not exactly the most centered one, but the maximal segment
containing the tangent at this element defined by Feschet and Tougne
\cite FeschetTougneDGCI1999 (definition 3): the symmetric tangent at an
element \f$ i \f$ is the segment \f$ [i-k, i+k] \f$ such that
\f$ [i-k-1, i+k+1] \f$ is not a segment, and it is extended as much as
possible at the front, then at the back. Since several maximal segments
may contain the symmetric tangent, the one with the largest index is
chosen.

\section modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators-Lambda Lambda-maximal segment estimator

LambdaMaximalSegmentEstimator assigns to each element a combination of
the quantities estimated from all the maximal segments passing through it
(\f$\lambda\f$-MST estimator \cite LachaudIVC2007). Let \f$ M_i \f$ be
the maximal segments passing through an element \f$ k \f$,
\f$ q_i(k) \f$ the quantity estimated at \f$ k \f$ from \f$ M_i \f$, and
\f$ e_i(k) \f$ the eccentricity of \f$ k \f$ in \f$ M_i \f$, i.e. its
relative position between the extremities of \f$ M_i \f$. The estimated
quantity at \f$ k \f$ is
\f[ \frac{\sum_i \lambda(e_i(k)) q_i(k)}{\sum_i \lambda(e_i(k))}, \f]
where \f$ \lambda \f$ is a mapping from \f$ [0,1] \f$ to \f$ \mathbb{R}^+ \f$
with \f$ \lambda(0) = \lambda(1) = 0 \f$ and \f$ \lambda > 0 \f$ elsewhere,
e.g. functors::Lambda64Function (default), functors::LambdaSinFromPiFunction,
functors::LambdaExponentialFunction or functors::LambdaTriangleFunction
(see FunctorsLambdaMST.h).

The eccentricity of an element only depends on its index in the segment
and on the number of elements of the segment. It is given by a functor,
which depends on the nature of the elements:
- functors::PointEccentricity (default) if the elements are points, as in
  \cite LachaudIVC2007 (e.g. the points of a GridCurve, used by
  ArithmeticalDSSComputer): the element of index \f$ i \f$ in a segment of
  \f$ n \f$ elements has eccentricity \f$ i/(n-1) \f$;
- functors::StepEccentricity if the elements are steps (e.g. the pairs of
  inner and outer points of a GridCurve, used by StabbingLineComputer and
  StabbingCircleComputer): the element of index \f$ i \f$ in a segment of
  \f$ n \f$ elements has eccentricity \f$ (i + 1/2)/n \f$.

@code
typedef StabbingCircleComputer<ConstCirculator> SegmentComputer;
typedef LambdaMaximalSegmentEstimator<SegmentComputer, SCEstimator,
  functors::LambdaTriangleFunction, functors::StepEccentricity> Estimator;
@endcode

Since the estimated quantities are averaged, they must form a vector space
(e.g. the unit vectors of TangentFromDSSEstimator) and be consistently
oriented along the curve. For instance, the normal vectors of
NormalFromDCAEstimator point toward the center of the separating circle,
i.e. toward the inside of the curve on its convex parts but toward the
outside on its concave parts: they must be reoriented, otherwise they
cancel out around the inflexion points (see the benchmark below).

@note LambdaMST2D is another implementation of the \f$\lambda\f$-MST
estimator, which takes a segmentation and evaluates the tangent at a given
point.

\section modulePrimitiveBasedEstimators-Benchmark Comparison

The benchmark normalEstimators-benchmark.cpp studies the multigrid
convergence and the running times of both estimators for the normal
vectors computed from maximal DSSs (ArithmeticalDSSComputer, on points),
from the maximal segments of StabbingLineComputer (on steps) and from the
maximal DCAs of StabbingCircleComputer (on steps), along the boundaries of
a digitized ellipse and of a digitized flower, for grid steps \f$ h \f$
from 1 to 0.0005. The error is the angle between the estimated and the true
normal directions.

All the estimators converge. The mean angular error of the estimators
based on straight segments decreases roughly as \f$ h^{2/3} \f$, and their
maximal error roughly as \f$ h^{1/3} \f$, whereas the errors of the
estimators based on circular arcs decrease faster (roughly as \f$ h \f$
for the mean error). The \f$\lambda\f$-MST estimators are 1.5 to 2.5 times
more accurate than the most centered ones, for a running time 1.4 to 3
times longer. The estimators based on circular arcs are the most accurate
ones, but they are about 20 to 60 times slower than the estimators based on
DSSs.

\image html normals-convergence-ellipse.png "Mean angular error (radian) of the normal estimators along the boundary of a digitized ellipse as a function of the grid step h." width=70%
